Put the session's account into the `WHERE` clause of each `UPDATE`, and return 404 when nothing matches. Three handlers need this: `PATCH /accounts/:id` (`routes/accounts.js:6`), `DELETE /accounts/:id` (`routes/accounts.js:14`), and `PATCH /orders/:id/cancel` (`routes/orders.js:14`). `sessionUser` already sets `req.user.accountId` on every request (`auth.js:11`), and `POST /orders` already uses it (`orders.js:9`).

Scope inside the UPDATE rather than running a SELECT first. A separate check-then-update can race, and a single statement is atomic.

`routes/accounts.js`:

```js
router.patch("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET display_name = $1, billing_email = $2 WHERE id = $3 AND id = $4 RETURNING *",
    [req.body.display_name, req.body.billing_email, req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});

router.delete("/:id", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE accounts SET deleted_at = now() WHERE id = $1 AND id = $2 RETURNING id",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.status(204).end();
});
```

`routes/orders.js`:

```js
router.patch("/:id/cancel", async (req, res) => {
  const row = await db.write(
    "UPDATE orders SET status = 'cancelled' WHERE id = $1 AND account_id = $2 RETURNING *",
    [req.params.id, req.user.accountId]
  );
  if (!row) return res.status(404).end();
  res.json(row);
});
```

The `!row` checks matter. `write()` returns `res.rows[0]`, so a zero-row match gives `undefined`. Today that means `res.json(undefined)` returns a 200 with an empty body, and DELETE returns 204 whether or not a row changed. Once the scope is in the query, a cross-account request will hit that path routinely. Returning 404 rather than 403 also avoids revealing whether the id exists.

I assumed `accounts.id` is the same value stored in `users.account_id` and `orders.account_id`. The repo has no schema or migrations, so check that first. If the values differ, the accounts predicates are wrong.

To test, log in as account A and PATCH, DELETE, and cancel account B's records. Each should return 404 and leave the row unchanged.

Enforce it in `sessionUser` (`auth.js:6`). Every request passes through that query, so one join blocks sessions for deleted accounts, and no route needs changes. Those sessions get the same 401 as a logged-out user.

```js
const rows = await db.query(
  "SELECT u.id, u.email, u.account_id FROM sessions s JOIN users u ON u.id = s.user_id JOIN accounts a ON a.id = u.account_id WHERE s.token = $1 AND s.expires_at > now() AND a.deleted_at IS NULL",
  [req.cookies && req.cookies.session]
);
```

Today a deleted account stays fully live. Its existing sessions still authenticate, and the accounts and orders routes still act on it. Nothing in the repo reads `deleted_at`. Only `routes/accounts.js:15` writes it.

Add one middleware at the top of `app.js`. It covers every route, including the 401s from `sessionUser`, so the routers need no edits.

```js
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const start = Date.now();
  res.on("finish", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      url: req.originalUrl,
      status: res.statusCode,
      ms: Date.now() - start,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
    }));
  });
  next();
});
```

Put it above `app.use(express.json())` so malformed-JSON requests get a line too. It reads `req.user` in the `finish` callback, after `sessionUser` has set it. I used `console.log` because the repo has no logging library, and there is no `package.json` to show one is installed.

Keep these out of the log: `req.body`, which holds `billing_email` and `display_name`; `req.cookies.session`, which is a live session token; and `req.user.email`.

The middleware has one gap. A request whose handler rejects without responding never reaches `finish`, so it gets no line. That is the `write()` failure path from earlier, and closing the gap means changing the error handling, not this middleware.

