Three details matter:

1. Put the scope in the same statement as the write. A separate SELECT-then-UPDATE leaves a race and costs an extra round trip.
2. `db.write` returns `rows[0]`, so a miss is `undefined`. Today `res.json(undefined)` sends an empty 200, and DELETE always sends 204. Without the `if (!row)` check, a scoped miss looks like success.
