Poll from the browser. Push isn't available on this platform, and the data changes only once a minute, so a short poll loses little. Only `public/dashboard.js` changes. `api/metrics.js` already returns `computed_at` and isn't cached.

**Why not push:** Functions are killed after 10 seconds, WebSocket upgrades return 501, and instances share no memory, so nothing can hold a connection open or fan out an update.

**How to build it:**

1. Poll every 15 seconds. Data is then at most about 15 seconds plus the job's runtime behind. A fixed interval is safer than lining polls up with the minute, because nothing tells us when the job finishes. That is 4 requests a minute per open tab, each about 30 ms.
2. Re-render only when `computed_at` changes, which avoids flicker and wasted work.
3. Ignore responses without `computed_at`.
4. Skip polls while the tab is hidden.

```js
let lastComputedAt = null;

async function load() {
  const res = await fetch('/api/metrics');
  const { rows, computed_at } = await res.json();
  if (!computed_at || computed_at === lastComputedAt) return;
  lastComputedAt = computed_at;
  render(rows);
  document.getElementById('stamp').textContent = new Date(computed_at).toLocaleTimeString();
}

document.getElementById('refresh').addEventListener('click', load);
window.addEventListener('load', () => {
  load();
  setInterval(() => { if (!document.hidden) load(); }, 15000);
});
```

